## How the project is structured

The tests are **keyword-driven** and follow the **Screenplay pattern**: every layer has one job and only uses
the layer below. At the top is **what** happens, at the bottom **how** it works technically. Everything
except the tests lives in `resources/`. All imports start with `${EXECDIR}` (= project folder), e.g.
`Resource    ${EXECDIR}/resources/tasks/web_tasks.resource`.

```text
tests/                   test cases
  │  import only
  ▼
resources/tasks/         business actions, e.g.  Log In
resources/assertions/    generic checks, e.g.    Element Should Show Text    ${MAIN_TITLE}    Products
  │  import
  ▼
resources/interactions/  technical helper keywords (tag "interaction") - the only place with library keywords
  │  import
  ▼
resources/abilities/     abilities: import one library each + its settings (no keywords)
resources/pages/         page objects: selectors per page, e.g. login_page.resource
  │
  ▼
resources/main.resource  standard libraries, CryptoLibrary, environments, credentials, global values
```

| Layer | Content | Used in tests |
|-------|---------|---------------|
| `tests/` | test cases built from tasks and assertions | - |
| `resources/tasks/` | business actions with arguments, e.g. `Add Product To Cart    sauce-labs-backpack` | yes |
| `resources/assertions/` | generic checks, e.g. `Number Of Elements Should Be`, `Status Code Should Be` | yes |
| `resources/interactions/` | technical steps, e.g. `Fill Field`, `Run Sql` - tagged `interaction` | no |
| `resources/pages/` | selectors of one page, names `PAGE_ELEMENT`, preferably `id=…` or `css=[data-test="…"]` | selectors only |
| `resources/abilities/` | `Library` import and settings such as server addresses | - |
| `resources/main.resource` | foundation for everything (see [environments](#environments-and-credentials)) | - |

`resources/assertions/core_assertions.resource` is always included and contains generic checks such as
`Value Should Be Filled`, `Value Should Equal` and `Text Should Include`.

**Interactions are marked:** every interaction file has `Keyword Tags    interaction` in its settings. That
way it is clear at a glance (also in RobotCode and in the log) which keywords belong in a test - tasks and
assertions - and which do not.

More files:

| File / folder | Purpose |
|---------------|---------|
| `robot.toml` | profiles for running - identical locally, in VS Code and in CI |
| `pyproject.toml`, `uv.lock` | Python packages with fixed versions |
| `data/keys/` | keys of the CryptoLibrary (the private key is not committed) |
{%- if include_doctest %}
| `data/reference/` | reference images and PDFs ("this is how it should look") |
{%- endif %}
| `.agents/skills/` | Agent Skills for coding assistants: project knowledge, writing tests, explaining results - no internal data |
| `results/` | results of the test runs, not committed |
| `.copier-answers.yml` | answers at creation and origin of the template - for `copier update` |

**Smaller project or a different style?** The layers are a suggestion. They can be merged into a basic
structure of keywords, pages and tests (`resources/keywords/<area>.resource` instead of abilities, interactions,
tasks and assertions), and other structures - e.g. by feature or page objects only - work as well. See the
template documentation, "Simpler structure for small projects" (`docs/generated-projects.md`).

> The folder `tasks/` contains reusable actions (keywords). It has nothing to do with the Robot Framework
> section `*** Tasks ***` for process automation.

## Setup and teardown

Preparing and cleaning up steps - starting and closing the browser, connecting to a database or a mailbox -
are **tasks**. That way they read well in the test:

```robotframework
*** Settings ***
Test Setup          Start Browser        # before every test
Test Teardown       Stop Browser         # after every test, even if it fails
```

- `Test Setup` / `Test Teardown` apply to every single test (every test starts clean).
- `Suite Setup` / `Suite Teardown` apply once for the whole file (faster, tests share the state).
- The technology behind it (e.g. `Launch Browser`) lives in the interactions, the settings (browser, window
  size, speed) in `main.resource`. The abilities stay without keywords.

## Running tests: profiles

All settings for running live in `robot.toml` as **profiles**. A profile is a named set of settings - such as
"environment test" or "only check syntax". Choose profiles with `-p`; several can be combined, later ones
override earlier ones. Without `-p`, `dev` applies.

```bash
uv run robotcode [-p <profile> ...] robot [file or folder] [-t "Name Of The Test"]
```

| Profile | Effect |
|---------|--------|
| `dev` | environment dev (default) |
| `test` | environment test |
| `prod` | environment prod - read-only tests |
| `smoke` | only quick core tests (tags `smoke` or `hello-world`) |
| `check` | only check syntax and imports, run nothing - also for otherwise excluded tests |
{%- if include_web or include_accessibility %}
| `headed` | visible browser with a 0.5 s pause after every action - to watch |
{%- endif %}
{%- if include_desktop %}
| `desktop` | only desktop tests (Windows) |
{%- endif %}
{%- if include_mobile %}
| `mobile` | only mobile tests (Appium server and Android device or emulator) |
{%- endif %}
{%- if include_email %}
| `email` | only e-mail tests (enter your mailbox) |
{%- endif %}
{%- if include_database and database_driver != 'sqlite' %}
| `database` | only tests against your own database |
{%- endif %}
{%- if include_sap %}
| `sap` | only SAP tests (Windows with a logged-in SAP GUI) |
{%- endif %}
| `ci` | for CI pipelines (GitLab CI, GitHub Actions): sets `${CI}`, writes a JUnit report |

Examples:

```bash
uv run robotcode -p test robot                     # all tests against the environment test
uv run robotcode -p test -p smoke robot            # only core tests against test
uv run robotcode robot tests/core                  # only one folder
uv run robotcode robot -t "Name Of The Test"       # a single test
uv run robotcode profiles list                     # show all profiles with description
```

This is what a profile looks like - create your own profiles the same way:

```toml
[profiles.test]
description = "Environment test"
variables = { ENV = "test" }          # sets ${ENV} - main.resource uses it to read URLS and LOGINS

[profiles.smoke]
description = "Only quick core tests"
includes = ["smoke", "hello-world"]   # only tests with these tags
```

More keys (e.g. `excludes`, `output-dir`, `dry-run`) are explained in the
[RobotCode documentation on robot.toml](https://robotcode.io/03_reference/config). In VS Code, choose the
profile in the test view via the gear icon.
{%- if include_web or include_accessibility %}

**Speed:** If the browser is too fast to watch, use `-p headed` (0.5 s pause after every action) or set
`${BROWSER_SPEED}` in `resources/main.resource` permanently, e.g. `1s`.
{%- endif %}

## Environments and credentials

Everything that differs between the environments lives in `resources/main.resource`:

```robotframework
${ENV}              dev                                   # active environment (profile -p dev/test/prod)
&{URLS_TEST}        web=https://test.example.com    api=https://api.test.example.com
&{LOGIN_TEST}       username=test-user    password=crypt:AbC...
```

They are read with `${URLS}[${ENV}][web]` and `${LOGINS}[${ENV}][username]`. Starting with `-p test`
automatically uses the values of the test environment.
{%- if include_web or include_accessibility %}
The preset credentials are the public demo data of saucedemo.com - replace them with your own.
{%- endif %}

**Passwords are never stored in plain text** in files or in Git. That is what the
[CryptoLibrary](https://github.com/Snooz82/robotframework-crypto) is for; its keys live in `data/keys/`:

1. Create a key pair once: `uv run CryptoLibrary` → *Open config* → folder `data/keys` → *Generate key pair*.
2. Encrypt a password: `uv run CryptoLibrary` → *Encrypt*. The result is a value like `crypt:AbC…`.
3. Enter the value in `resources/main.resource`. It is decrypted automatically when the tests start.
4. The **private** key (`data/keys/private_key*`) stays local (it is in `.gitignore`) and is stored as a
   protected variable in CI. The public key may be committed.

## Reading the results

After every run, `results/` contains:

- **`report.html`** - overview: how many tests passed or failed.
- **`log.html`** - every step of every test with error messages{% if include_web %} and screenshots{% endif %}.
- **`output.xml`** - machine-readable, basis for reports and CI.
{%- if include_accessibility %}
- **`accessibility/`** - one accessibility report per checked page (linked in the log).
{%- endif %}

In the log, expand the failed test and follow the red path to the first failed step. The message there is
almost always the actual cause.

## Writing your first own test

1. **Write the flow in words:** what is given, what does one do, what should come out?
2. **Look for existing building blocks** in `resources/tasks/` and `resources/assertions/`.
3. **Add what is missing - bottom up:**
   - new selectors in `resources/pages/` (one file per page, e.g. `profile_page.resource`)
   - new technical steps in `resources/interactions/` (only if none fits)
   - the action in `resources/tasks/`, a missing generic check in `resources/assertions/`
4. **Create the test case** in `tests/<area>/` as a sequence of tasks and assertions:

   ```robotframework
   *** Settings ***
{%- if include_web %}
   Resource            ${EXECDIR}/resources/tasks/web_tasks.resource
   Resource            ${EXECDIR}/resources/assertions/web_assertions.resource
   Test Setup          Start Browser
   Test Teardown       Stop Browser
{%- else %}
   Resource            ${EXECDIR}/resources/tasks/core_tasks.resource
   Resource            ${EXECDIR}/resources/assertions/core_assertions.resource
{%- endif %}

   *** Test Cases ***
   Name Describes The Expected Behaviour
       [Documentation]    What is checked and why.
       [Tags]    smoke
{%- if include_web %}
       Open Login Page
       Log In
       Add Product To Cart    sauce-labs-bike-light
       Element Should Show Text    ${MAIN_CART_BADGE}    1
{%- else %}
       ${login}    Read Credentials Of Environment    test
       Credentials Should Be Complete    ${login}
{%- endif %}
   ```

5. **Check and run:**

   ```bash
   uv run robocop format
   uv run robocop check
   uv run robotcode robot -t "Name Describes The Expected Behaviour"
   ```

Good test cases have a name that describes the expected behaviour, are independent of each other, never wait
with `Sleep` and carry tags (e.g. `smoke` for quick core tests). The skill `robot-write-test` helps a coding
assistant to suggest test cases in exactly this style (see [Coding assistants](#coding-assistants-agent-skills)).

## Coding assistants (Agent Skills)

The folder `.agents/skills/` contains three **Agent Skills** - instructions in the open
[Agent Skills](https://agentskills.io/) format that a coding assistant loads when needed. They only contain
the project structure, rules, existing building blocks and links to public documentation, **no internal or
personal data**.

| Skill | Used for |
|-------|----------|
| `robot-project` | structure, installed libraries, commands and rules of this project |
| `robot-write-test` | business test idea → keyword-driven Robot Framework test with the existing tasks and assertions |
| `robot-explain-results` | run tests, explain results and error messages in plain words |

**How to use them:**

1. Open the project folder in a coding assistant that supports Agent Skills. Many tools find
   `.agents/skills/` automatically.
2. If your tool expects a different folder (e.g. `.github/skills/` or a folder in your home directory), copy
   the three skill folders there. Every folder contains a `SKILL.md`.
3. Just ask - the assistant picks the matching skill by its description, e.g.
   "Write a test: login with a wrong password shows an error message" or
   "Why did the test *Product Lands In The Cart* fail?". Many tools also allow calling a skill by name, e.g.
   `/robot-write-test`.
4. **In a web chat without file access** (e.g. a company chat assistant in the browser):
   - Open `.agents/skills/<skill>/SKILL.md` in this project and copy the **whole** content.
   - Paste it as custom instructions / system prompt / project knowledge if the chat offers that, otherwise as
     the first message with "Use these instructions for this conversation." For general help paste all three.
   - Ask your question or paste a failure message from `results/log.html`.
   - If the assistant asks for a file, paste just that file. It answers with complete blocks per file path -
     copy them into the project and run the commands it names.
   - Never paste credentials or real personal data. After adding keywords or libraries, paste the skills again.

   Every skill is self-contained - structure, rules and existing keywords are in the text.

The skills are written so that the assistant asks as little as possible: it looks up missing information in
the project, makes sensible assumptions and states them. You can add your own, non-confidential notes (e.g.
names of test environments) to `.agents/skills/robot-project/SKILL.md`.

## Adding more libraries

Libraries that were not chosen at creation can be added at any time. Matching libraries can be found at
[MarketSquare](https://github.com/MarketSquare), the GitHub organisation of the Robot Framework community
(e.g. Browser Library, RequestsLibrary, DatabaseLibrary), and in the overview at
<https://robotframework.org/#resources>.
{%- if own_library_areas %}
{%- set area_names = {'web': 'web', 'api': 'API', 'database': 'database'} %}

> **Own library planned:** at creation, a different or own library was chosen for
> {% for area in own_library_areas %}**{{ area_names[area] }}**{{ ', ' if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}.
> There are no example files for it yet - add it following the steps below.
{%- endif %}

They are added following the same pattern:

1. Install the package: `uv add <package>`. Package names differ from library to library - many start with
   `robotframework-` (e.g. `robotframework-sshlibrary`), others do not (e.g. `RESTinstance`). The exact name
   is on [PyPI](https://pypi.org/) or in the documentation of the library. uv chooses a version that matches
   Robot Framework {{ rf_version }} and enters it in `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock`.
2. Create an ability: `resources/abilities/<name>_ability.resource` with
   `Resource    ${EXECDIR}/resources/main.resource`, the `Library` line and the settings it needs.
3. Create interactions: `resources/interactions/<name>_interactions.resource` with
   `Keyword Tags    interaction` - wrap the keywords of the library into small helper steps here.
4. Add tasks and assertions, then write a first test.
5. Check: `uv run robotcode -p check robot` and `uv run robocop check`.
6. Add the library with its documentation link to `.agents/skills/robot-project/SKILL.md` (table
   "Installed libraries") so that coding assistants know it.

## Notes on the libraries

**Basics** - `tests/core/` shows how credentials are read per environment and passwords are hidden in the log
with the CryptoLibrary.
{%- if include_web or include_accessibility %}

### Web{% if include_web %} ({{ 'Browser Library' if web_library == 'browser' else 'SeleniumLibrary' }}){% endif %}

The examples use the demo shop [saucedemo.com](https://www.saucedemo.com): log in, add a product to the cart,
log out. The page objects live in `resources/pages/`: `login_page`, `main_page` (product overview),
`cart_page` (cart) and `settings_page` (menu). New pages get their own file, e.g. `profile_page.resource`.

Selectors from stable to fragile: **ID** (`id=login-button`) → **test attribute** (`css=[data-test="title"]`)
→ role or text → CSS classes → XPath only as a last resort. Find them with the developer tools of the browser
(F12 → right click → *Inspect*).
{%- if include_web and web_library == 'selenium' %}
The SeleniumLibrary does not wait automatically - so the interactions wait before they click or read.
It uses Google Chrome; Selenium downloads the matching driver itself.
{%- else %}
The Browser Library waits automatically until an element is ready.
{%- endif %}
{%- if include_accessibility and include_web and web_library == 'browser' %}
Web tests and accessibility share the Browser Library - it is imported only once
(`resources/abilities/browser_ability.resource`).
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}
{%- if include_api %}

### API ({{ 'RequestsLibrary' if api_library == 'requests' else 'RESTinstance' }})

The example uses the open test API [jsonplaceholder.typicode.com](https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/)
(no login). The address per environment is in `main.resource` (`URLS`), the endpoints in
`resources/abilities/api_ability.resource`. Every request returns a dictionary with `status` and `body`,
no matter which library works underneath.
{%- endif %}
{%- if include_database %}

### Database (DatabaseLibrary)

The examples create customers in an in-memory SQLite database - no server needed. Its connection data is in
`resources/abilities/database_ability.resource` (`EXAMPLE_DB_…`).
{%- if database_driver != 'sqlite' %}
The driver for your own {{ 'PostgreSQL' if database_driver == 'postgresql' else 'MySQL/MariaDB' }} database is installed. Server, port and database name are in
the same file (`DB_…`), user and password in `main.resource` (`LOGINS`). The connection test runs with
`uv run robotcode -p database robot`.
{%- else %}
For a database server, add the driver: `uv add psycopg2-binary` (PostgreSQL) or `uv add pymysql`
(MySQL/MariaDB), then enter the connection data in the ability.
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}
{%- if include_desktop %}

### Desktop (PlatynUI)

The example calculates in the Windows calculator. Windows and elements are Python classes in the page object
`resources/pages/calculator_page.py`. Find the details of new elements with `uv run PlatynUI.Spy`. Window names
depend on the Windows language ("Calculator", "Rechner" ...). PlatynUI is still in development.
{%- endif %}
{%- if include_mobile %}

### Mobile (AppiumLibrary)

Requires a running [Appium server](https://appium.io/docs/en/latest/quickstart/) and an Android device or
emulator. Selectors are in `resources/pages/android_settings_page.resource` (order: `accessibility_id=…`,
`id=…`, `android=new UiSelector()…`). Find them with the
[Appium Inspector](https://github.com/appium/appium-inspector).
{%- endif %}
{%- if include_faker %}

### Test data (FakerLibrary)

Generates fake but realistic data. Language and country are set in
`resources/abilities/faker_ability.resource` (`${FAKER_LOCALE}`, default `en_US`).
{%- endif %}
{%- if include_email %}

### E-mail (ImapLibrary2)

Waits for e-mails in an IMAP mailbox and checks text and links. Server and port are in
`resources/abilities/email_ability.resource`, user and password in `main.resource`.
The example test runs with `uv run robotcode -p email robot` once a mailbox is entered.
{%- endif %}
{%- if include_doctest %}

### Images and PDFs (DocTestLibrary)

Compares images and PDFs with reference files in `data/reference/` and shows differences in the log.
A new reference image changes what counts as "correct" - only replace it on purpose.
{%- endif %}
{%- if include_sap %}

### SAP (RoboSAPiens)

Operates SAP GUI via visible labels, e.g. `Fill Text Field    Material    4711`.
Runs only on Windows with an open and logged-in SAP GUI. Window titles depend on the SAP GUI language.
{%- endif %}
{%- if include_accessibility %}

### Accessibility (axe-core)

Uses the Browser Library (installed for it, but imported only once).

- `tests/accessibility/without_login.robot` - **data-driven**: every row is a public page.
- `tests/accessibility/with_login.robot` - logs in and checks pages behind it (product overview, cart). The
  login uses the page objects and the credentials from `main.resource`.

An HTML report is written for every page to `results/accessibility/`. Which impacts make a test fail is set
in `resources/abilities/browser_ability.resource` (`A11Y_BLOCKING_IMPACTS`). Automated checks find only part
of the problems and do not replace a manual review.
{%- endif %}
{%- if include_gitlab_ci %}

## GitLab CI

The pipeline in `.gitlab-ci.yml` uses the same profiles as locally:

| Job | Command | When |
|-----|---------|------|
| `check` | Robocop and `robotcode -p ci -p check robot` | on every push |
| `test` | `robotcode -p ci robot` | on every push |
| `test-environment` | `robotcode -p ci -p $RF_ENV -p smoke robot` | scheduled, when `RF_ENV` is set |
| `template-check` | compares the project with the branch `rf-{{ rf_version }}` of the template | on the default branch; yellow (hint only) when there is something new - see [Updating from the template](#updating-from-the-template) |

**Image:** the default is [`ppodgorsek/robot-framework`](https://hub.docker.com/r/ppodgorsek/robot-framework) -
it ships browsers, drivers and Node.js. The pipeline installs `uv` in it and uses the project environment
from `uv.lock`, so that the same versions run locally and in CI. Alternatives are listed as comments in
`.gitlab-ci.yml` (e.g. a slim `uv` image for API and database tests).

For scheduled runs, set the variable `RF_ENV` (e.g. `test`) under *Build → Pipeline schedules*. Store the
private CryptoLibrary key as a protected variable.
{%- endif %}
